## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical testing design for event-driven pipelines, focusing on end-to-end integrity, exactly-once semantics, dead-letter queues, and resilience under burst traffic—common challenges in fintech and ride-hailing domains.

## Key Concepts

- Event-driven architecture
- Exactly-once processing and idempotency
- Dead-letter queues and backpressure
- Failure injection and chaos testing
- Data integrity validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: ensure idempotent consumer
let lastProcessed = new Map();
async function process(event) {
  const key = event.id;
  if (lastProcessed.has(key)) {
    return { status: 'duplicate', result: lastProcessed.get(key) };
  }
  const result = await processEvent(event);
  lastProcessed.set(key, result);
  return { status: 'processed', result };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to design practical tests for eventual consistency in offline-first scenarios, including merge behavior, conflict resolution policy, and data integrity under CI.

## Key Concepts

- Offline-first and eventual consistency
- Merge policies (last-writer-wins, deterministic merges, or CRDTs)
- Conflict scenarios and data integrity checks
- Deterministic test datasets and network partition simulations
- CI integration, test isolation, and seed data management

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example Jest-like test (pseudo)
describe('offline sync', () => {
  test('merges offline edits on reconnect using last-writer-wins', async () => {
    const server = new MockServer();
    const clientA = new Client(server, { offline: true });

    server.createNote({ id: 'n1', content: 'Hello' });

    // offline edit on client A
    clientA.createNoteOffline({ id: 'n1', content: 'Hello world' });

    // reconnect and sync
    await clientA.sync();

    // verify on server
    expect(server.getNote('n1').content).toBe('Hello world');
  });
});
```

